Mark Paede wrote:
> 
> So the moving of partitions would still be interesting, but it seems
> that it doesn't work.

The only way to move the partition is to backup, destroy, create,
restore.

> Another question: It doesn't make sense for me to use a, b and g as so
> c-f are lost (especially with my pre-install where 2GB are left free
> behind g). Is it a need for DU to use this partitions? And if yes -
> why? (But this will be probably unanswered.)

The idea behind this partitioning scheme is to have multi-purpose
default disk labels (part.). You just "splash" the default and you have
several choices. If it is a system disk, then use {a-root, b-swap,
g-usr, h-var} or {a-root, b-swap, advfs:(g+h)-usr/var}. Or if you want
it as a whole, then use c. And so on.

That is why I always make my custom disklabel, when I install the
system. I know exactly what I want, swap space specially.

Nix.



