Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hi.

Every time we get a new layered products CD, we have to go through the
installed software products and upgrade the ones that we previously
installed if the version on the CD is newer.  We have been doing this
by a laborious manual process of

  1. ``setld -i | grep installed'' to inventory what products
     are installed and what version each one is.

  2. Page through the Software Product Library contents listing to
     find these products and see if the ones on the CD are later
     versions.

  3. ``setld -d subset...'' to delete the subsets we are going to
     upgrade.  (Otherwise step 4 will fail.)

  4. ``setld -l /mnt/subset/kit'' to load the new subsets.

This is not only tedious but rather error-prone.  Having used RedHat
Package Manager to upgrade Linux software, I am struck by how
stone-age setld is, and I can't help wondering why it cannot do at
least a one of the following things:

   -- Automatically compare the installed optional software with the
      ones on the CD to tell you what needs to be upgraded.

   -- Automatically delete earlier versions of installed software when
      later ones are installed, instead of complaining.

Or did I overlook something in the man page?

-- 

