I found this whole conversation rather entertaining.  I am going to propose
a few interesting concepts that most techies never contemplate.

1.)  Published results are for looks of that specific company only!  Even
the benchmarkers will tell you that.  goto www.specs.org.  It will set you
straight.  PLUS there are a lot that goes towards a specific benchmark
anyways.  Has anyone taken a statistics course?  Do yall know who makes
these benchmarks?  In a stats. course you will learn that there are many
bias results.  For example, one machine may have 512MB RAM and the other
machine will have 64MB RAM and the benchmark performed needs a lot of memory
therefore the machine w/ more memory appears faster but in all actuality may
not be.

2.)  I also read a exerpt about that DEC are cheaper.  That is what Compaq
(DEC) might want you to believe.  Most of the time you pay for what you get!
The TCO may be just the contrary.  This is where one must look very hard at
what they need and which vender fullfills those needs.  I will quote Merrill
Lynch in a summaries fashion that IBM & AIX seem to be the long term people
to bet your busiess w/ on the UNIX side of things.  PS  Did you know that
IBM is a 800 BILLION dollar company.  The next closest company to IBM is
hmmm. Compaq with its 30 Billion including its recent struggling DEC
aquision.  Besides would you want to bet on a Alpha chip now that intel owns
it?  Can you say discontiued?

3.)  Just a little end note here.  BRAVO to the guy that said stuff about
clock speeds and OS's.  I admired you!  Every machine and every clock speed
doesn't automatically put you in the realm of others w/ the same specs.  If
so all the UNIX guys would have been out of business a lot time ago.  In
fact, in a server or high end workstation the processor speed is the least
of your concern its I/O capability.  How fast is your machine w/ I/O.  For
example, how fast is your bus speed if you have one.  I ass/u/me that you
know that IBM's high end machines don't use a bus.  If not tough I don't
want to explain it.
PS  The 43P isn't a dinosaur either.  Its still being made today the 43P-240
is living and kicking butt!  In fact there is no one around keeping up and I
am talking real time here.  We own A LOT OF UNIX BOX's!  In fact I sell all
of them so I know the up and downs extremely well.

If yall have any complaints to what I may have said please email me and
state why you don't like it.  I am not meaning to offend anyone!  I just
have my OPINIONS and thats what they are.  I am more than willing to change
them though!

Stan " The Unix Man" Rock

