Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hi there

I tried to set up a new (remote) printer using lprsetup, say,
"test1_var". Since I don't want the standard settings for the spooling
directory (sd) and printer error file (lf), I enter
"/var/spool/test1_var" and "/var/adm/lpd_err/test1_var", respectively.
After entering all information, lprsetup reports the following errors:

--- start output ------
lprsetup not allowed to chmod(/var/spool/test1_var, 0755)

Cannot chmod /var/spool/test1_var to mode 0755
/var/spool/test1_var: Read-only file system
lprsetup not allowed to chmod(/var/adm/lpd_err/test1_var, 0644)

Cannot chmod /var/adm/lpd_err/test1_var to mode 0644
/var/adm/lpd_err/test1_var: Read-only file system
--- end output ------

The strange thing is, when I set up a printer "test1_usr", where I only
replace "/var" with "/usr", it works just fine. No errors, nothing. The
files are created in exactly the same place, since /var is just a link
to /usr/var.

A listing of the files created: (with ls -ld)
-rw-r--r--   1 daemon   daemon         0 Nov 23 10:59
/var/adm/lpd_err/test1_usr
-rw-r--r--   1 root     adm            0 Nov 23 11:01
/var/adm/lpd_err/test1_var
drwxr-xr-x   2 daemon   daemon      8192 Nov 23 11:12
/var/spool/test1_usr
drwxr-xr-x   2 root     system      8192 Nov 23 11:12
/var/spool/test1_var

So, when I use /var instead of /usr, lprsetup obviously can't chmod the
created files to daemon:daemon.

Does anyone know of this problem? Or even how to fix it?

Thanks for any replies!

Urs

