Well, you've gotten all the info you needed on SMB stuff. Many people I know tend
to prefer samba over ASDU. On the Apple side of things, I use (and install for
customers) a software kit called Helios Ethershare. It emulates an
AppleShareIP-compliant AFP server on the Digital Unix box. It's rather expensive
(a few thousands of dollars for the base license of 20 Macintosh connections) but
offers Macintosh-based management of the unix box and *stellar* performance. It is
also extremely robust.

If you need to go shareware or freeeware, there might be a version of Columbia
AppleTalk (cap) for Digital Unix, I'm not sure. Last time I used it on Sun, I got
sligtly discouraged at the patch installation process (over 200 patches). You
actually *need* some/all of the patches to support one or the other newer versions
of MacOS, fast Ethernet, etc.. Very long, tedious and flaky performance.

Hope this helps.

