In article <363DD93E.4ED63BA4@bgl.psycha.upenn.edu>,
  Nicholas Guido <nick@bgl.psycha.upenn.edu> wrote:
> I am trying to backup the entire system for a Dec alpha station 600.  I
> used the command:
>
> vdump -0uNC /
>
> thinking this would start from root and continue to backup throughout.
> However this is not right.  What do I put as the "file system name"
> instead of "/" to get it to back up the entire operationg system and all
> susequent files and subdirectories?  I appreciate any help

I don't think you can vdump all the filesystems by just specifying root. If
you do a df to list all the filesystems, ignoring proc. Then do something
like: vdump -0uNC / vdump -0uNC /usr vdump -0uNC /tmp etc. for each
filesystem. To restore use vrestore -i -f /dev/nrmt0h. When the tape is first
loaded it will be at the first save set (root). To restore /usr, cd to /usr
and do: mt -f /dev/nrmt0h fsf 1 (to get to the second save set) and then:
vrestore -i -f /dev/nrmt0h. And so on, depending on how many filesystems you
backed up and want to restore.

Mark.

