Hi,

I have the following problem:

I run amanda (a freeware network backup) on a DEC Personal Workstation
with Digital Unix 4.0D (pl2). This needs some temporary diskspace to store
backups collected from clients over the network before writing them to a
tape. Some times it occurs, that amanda mis-estimates the backup sizes and
writes to the disk until it is 100% full. No problem, amanda recognizes
this and can recover from such a situation.

But the disk becomes unusable (I guess because of fragmentation) even when
the temporary backup files already have been removed ... write access
still reports "write failed, file system is full".

When I then try to defragment the following occurs:

root@alpha5-stat[/root]#  defragment -v usr2_domain
defragment: Defragmenting domain 'usr2_domain'
defragment: Can't get file set info for domain 'usr2_domain'
defragment: Error = ENO_SUCH_TAG (-1032)
defragment: Can't defragment domain 'usr2_domain'

But there is a 'usr2' file set in the 'usr2_domain':
root@alpha5-stat[/root]# df
Filesystem         1024-blocks  Used Available Capacity Mounted on
...
usr2_domain#usr2     2095112 1177042   856008     58%   /usr2

And there are over 800 MB free !! defragment works on other file domains.

The file domain has been created with the "-x 2048" option, so there
should be no problem with missing room for meta data on the file system.


Any ideas or hints? 

What does "Error = ENO_SUCH_TAG" (see above) mean?


Thanks,

Albrecht

