>On 2 Nov 98 16:37:58 GMT, Dan Pop <Dan.Pop@cern.ch> wrote:
>>In <slrn73k748.u48.soupjrc@emily.tampa.advantis.com> soupjrc@emily.tampa.advanti
>> s.com (John R. Campbell) writes:
>>
>>>	dealing with Linux.  One thing holding Linux back is the effort
>>>	to maintain some compatibility with MS-DOG's partitioning...
>>
>>If you want to boot the system from the hard disk, you MUST use the
>>partitioning scheme the BIOS itself understands.
>
>	Actually, the only thing the BIOS is really looking for is the
>	first sector (cyl0/trk0/sec1) on the disk, which, if the signature
>	is properly set, will be executed.  If the bootstrap code is
>	changed (like LILO) the existing partitioning structure could
>	easily become a non-issue.  Additional logic in the BIOS can
>	be safely ignored (I would think);  Just don't call it.
>
>	Unfortunately, this does not help the dual-booters-  They *have*
>	to maintain some semblance of the existing partition table so
>	that MS-DOG/Windoze knows where it "lives"...

And putting LILO in the MBR of a PC which is not Linux-only is asking
for troubles.  The only safe usage of LILO, if the machine also boots
some Windows flavour, is in the boot sector of a Linux partition.
Once you've put LILO in a boot sector of a partition, the partitioning
scheme must be BIOS compatible.

A Windows reinstallation/upgrade will wipe your MBR and put its own
instead.  So, if you had LILO installed there, you will not be able to
boot Linux from the hard disk any longer.  You'll have to find a rescue
diskette to boot the system and restore LILO on the MBR.  

LILO in the boot sector of a Linux partition is safe.  All that is
needed after a Windows reinstallation is to boot Windows and make the
Linux partition active (the Windows installation procedure will make
the Windows partition active, of course :-)

Dan
