Subject: Enhanced security and user{mod,del}
Date: 1998/11/03
Message-ID: <weber-0311981345480001@spinel.bucknell.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 408014608
Sender: weber@spinel.bucknell.edu
Organization: Bucknell University
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

  We recently upgraded an Alpha server to Digital UNIX 4.0D and turned on
enhanced security, primarily for its password-hiding function.  Instead of
editing /etc/passwd and running mkpasswd to create or delete
authorizations, our automated authorization process now runs  useradd 
and  userdel .

  When we switched from BASE to ENHANCED security mode, the ~5500 existent
authorizations were auto-migrated to their proper homes in whatever files
were appropriate.  This seems to have worked just swell, and I can modify
these accounts with  usermod  and retire them with  userdel .

  There's a problem, though.  Any authorization created *after* the
auto-migrate can't be modified by either  usermod  or  userdel .  Any
attempt to apply either of these applications to a post-4.0D authorization
results in the message:

                    Specified user not found


  edauth  has no problem finding, modifying, or deleting the auth.db entry
for any user about which  user{del,mod}  has barked, and  vipw  lets me
update the non-extended parts of a user profile, so apparently there's a
way around this.  However, I'd feel a lot better if the  user??? 
applications just worked their magic, particularly if they have to update
files about which I have no knowledge.

  I just installed the latest consolidated patch file for 4.0D (19980717)
in hopes that that would clear up the problem, but no dice.


  Can anyone shed any light on this odd behavior?  I'm perplexed.

  Thanks in advance for any help.

-- 
Chris Weber <weber@bucknell.edu>
Bucknell ISR/CCS



