In article <5lk91dqm3s.fsf@tequila.cs.yale.edu>,
Stefan Monnier  <monnier+comp/unix/admin/news/@tequila.cs.yale.edu> wrote:
>- is it possible/easy to have an LVM logical volume as your root/only
>  "partition" ?

I don't think this is possible (or at least advisable) yet.  One caveat is
that you could set up a very small root partition (with kernel, /etc, /lib,
and /sbin), and then have all of the other filesystems in LVM.  AIX does
this by having the boot filesystem (which MUST also be contiguous on disk).
Because you are using LVM, you really only need 2 DOS partitions to do this
(one for /, and one for LVM which can be divided into LVs as desired).

>- how easy is it to extend an ext2fs filesystem after extending the underlying
>  LV ?  Without unmounting the filesystem, of course.

According to T.Y.Tso (ext2 developer), it is possible to extend an ext2
filesystem from the current size up to the next 512MB boundary, but after
that you need to re-write parts of the filesystem, and the problem is non-
trivial.

>- any sign of getting the ext2fs shrinking done any time soon ?  And without
>  unmounting the filesystem ?

I would be happy enough for now to be able to do it when the FS is unmounted.
AIX still doesn't allow one to do it at all (I've heard that possible NFS
problems are the excuse for not allowing this - I'd accept this risk).

I'd be happy if someone would make a set of AIX compatibility commands
for Linux LVM, so I don't have to learn the HP-style syntax...

Cheers, Andreas
