I'm not 100% sure that I understand your question, so let me try
to restate it:

You would like to know whether there will be a performance cost
if you add a lot of IP address "aliases" to the interface(s)
on your system.

If you are using a recent version of Digital UNIX (such as V4.0D),
then the answer is in the Release Notes document:

    A.1.8    Networking Limits 

    IP alias addresses 

     DIGITAL UNIX allows the use of up to 5,000 IP alias addresses before
     system performance begins to degrade. 

You can get at the Release Notes document online, at
    http://www.UNIX.digital.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/
		DOCUMENTATION/V40D_HTML/AQTLMCTE/TITLE.HTM

Warning: if you are using a much older version of Digital UNIX,
you *will* encounter a performance degradation if you try to
use thousands of IP aliases.  I can't remember when the kernel
was changed to efficiently support a lot of aliases; you could
either check the release notes for the older systems, or just
upgrade to V4.0D (which has a lot of other performance advantages
for Web servers).

-Jeff



