Nikola Milutinovic wrote:

> Mark@mwsys.demon.co.uk wrote:
> >
> > Whilst looking at a password problem on a system I noticed some differences
> > in the Enhanced security files between two systems on the same OS version
> > (4.0D) but one was a fresh install while the other was an update install.
> > They have both got enhanced security enabled, on the fresh install system
> > there is the database (auth.db) in the /tcb/files directory but this does not
> > exist on the system which was updated and the information which is in the
> > auth.db (like the u_pwd and u_pwdict) are in the /tcb/files/auth/a-z
> > subdirectory for the user. (as was the case before version 4.0.) Does anybody
> > know why there is this difference between a new install and an update
> > install?
>
> Well, you've answered the question yourself. One was an upgrade install and it
> left the database in the old format (/tcb/files/auth/<letter>). The other was
> fresh install and it automtically used new (binary) format.
>
> You can perform conversions from one to another with command "convauth".
>
> Nix.

A "problem" which is documented in the release notes by the way...  Of course I
know no one actually reads the release notes but it is there!

In any case, this "problem" has been fixed for the 4.0E and all future releases
which will force the usages of the auth.db format over the file format...

since it wasn't mentioned specifically:

"convauth -d p" will take you from file to db format
"convauth -O" would get you back to file from db format

