Craig R Kisely wrote:
> > We are looking at ditching it
> > and moving the AIX workloads onto our SP.  It will save a lot of floor
> > space too, in the proceess.  The SP boxes from a hardware perspective are
> > really nothing to write home about.  They are really H50s.  Where SPs
> > are interesting is when you look at plugging LOTS of them in together.
> >
> 
> No.  An H50 is a rack F50, just as the R30[40]etc... series maps to the J class.
> The S70 is the 64 bit implementation for the new AIX4.3
> And SP2's are completely unique in their planners from any of the aforementioned
> rs6k classes.

I beg to differ.  Take an F50.  Rack mount it, and it's an H50.  Add an
SP backplane connector, its a 332 SMP node.  Take a J50.  Rack mount it,
and it's and R50.  Add an SP backplane connector, it's a 604e High node.
Take a 397, add an SP backplane connector, it's a 160 Thin node.  (I
think there used to be a rack mount version too, R24?)

It's the S70 that is a completely new and different animal.

-
geoff



