In article <1998Nov6.094550@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu>,
Craig Paul <paul@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu> wrote:
> On a very heavily loaded system (4.0) we're getting occasional mail
> file corruption. We've seen cases in which the system is so busy
> that PINE gets an error trying to close a very small mailbox and
> occasional mailboxes accessed via POP3 seems to get corrupted by
> "garbage" being written at the front (or very rarely) in the middle of
> a mailbox.

Yes, we've seen this problem, as have a lot of other people.  You might try
searching the archives for the `alpha-osf-managers' mailing list.  I posted
a question and then later a summary regarding this problem a year or more ago.

My theory is that it's a bug in `binmail' that's triggered when you fill your
/tmp or /var/tmp filesystem (whichever it is that binmail uses).

We've switched to using `mail.local' from the Sendmail distribution as the
local delivery agent on all our Digital Unix boxes, and we haven't seen the
problem since.  Some of our Digital machines are pretty busy, and we haven't
noticed any significant performance penalty by using `mail.local' instead of
`binmail'.

Tim
