In our site we have had no previous experience with automount (in any flavour).
From many sources, we got the impression that amd could have significant
advantages on other implementations. However, after many weeks of trial and
error and many re-readings of the manual (maybe a better amd-tutorial would be
nice!), we still have had only a partial  success. We do not understand whether
the problems come from our limited understanding of how amd works or from any
problem with our platform (Digital Unix 4.0d).

Our final goal is to have a uniform user namespace 
(say /home/user1, home/user2 ...) even if the actual user directories 
are physically spread over different hosts. We also would like to minimize the
number of mounted filesystems.

We have made experiments with amd as follows:
 
1)through amd we would like to access  the subdirectories of /tmp_tmp 
(residing on host1 and eventually containing all the user directories on host1)
as subdirectories of the /mnt mount point on host2 (eventually appearing 
as /home).

2)We would like to generate automatically the list of subdirectories 
(this is the reason for a wildcharacter key).

3)We would like to be able to browse the mounted subdirectories with commands 
like
(cd /mnt ; ls -l ).

Apparently we are able to achieve 1 and 2 with the following setup and line 
command but we cannot list the subdirectories through "ls /mnt" unless they 
have been already explicitely referred and the link has been created by amd 
from /mnt to the corresponding file in the real amd mountpoint ( for us 
/a/host1/tmp_tmp).

The doc is not very clear about this specific point. We interpreted the 
following section of the section on the parameters as saying that we have 
to use "full" instead of "yes" as browsable_dirs value. Without success.

---- from amd doc:

6.4.1 browsable_dirs Parameter

(type=string, default=`no'). If `yes', then Amd's top-level mount points will 
be browsable to readdir(3) calls. This means you could run for example ls(1) and 
see what keys are available
to mount in that directory. Not all entries are made visible to readdir(3): the 
`/defaults' entry, wildcard entries, and those with a `/' in them are not included. 
If you specify `full' to
this option, all but the `/defaults' entry will be visible. Note that if you run a 
command which will attempt to stat(2) the entries, such as often done by `ls -l' 
or `ls -F', Amd will
attempt to mount every entry in that map. This is often called a "mount storm". 
----


Any hints ?
Thanks in advance for any help.

                                     Giorgio Pastore



Configuration files:

---- amd.conf:

# GLOBAL OPTIONS SECTION (must be first in amd.conf file)
[ global ]
normalize_hostnames =         no
# (amd -p)
print_pid =			 no
restart_mounts =		yes
unmount_on_exit =		yes
#unmount_on_exit =		no
# (amd -a)
auto_dir =			/a
# duration in seconds that a looked up name remain cached (amd -c)
cache_duration =		300
# (amd -l)
log_file =			/var/adm/amd
log_options =			all
#plock =				 yes
# selectors on /defaults are off by default
selectors_on_default =		 yes
# should browsable maps show number of entries to df/statfs (default=no)
#show_statfs_entries =		 yes
# these 5 options can be overridden by each map individually
browsable_dirs =		full 
map_type =			file
mount_type =			nfs
search_path =			/etc/amdmaps
# Use fully qualified host names
fully_qualified_hosts =		yes


---- /etc/amdmaps/amd.map


/defaults    opts:=intr,nfsv3;type:=nfs
*	 -sublink:=${key};type:=nfs \
	rhost:=host1.ts.infn.it;rfs:=/tmp_tmp


---- Command to start amd (on host2):

amd -F /etc/amd.conf /mnt /etc/amdmaps/amd.map

