AND . . .

Price is more than just the cost of the box itself. I know this is an
overworked phrase, but TCO (Total Cost of Ownership) is THE way to
judge how much performance you will see in the real world. 

You have to look at the cost of the box, the OS (and possible add-ins
needed to make the comparison "apples to apples") the cost of support
AND its quality (a place where DEC and Compaq have some real
problems), costs of future expansion, administration costs (how many
person-hours per month will it take to keep the system running
correctly), costs of downtime (both real costs such as wages payed to
idle personnel and the intangable costs such as loss of customer
confidence), etc.

I can't speak too much on the Alphas, but as far as the RS/6000s, they
do shine in all the areas above: at least that has been the
experiences we've had where I work. In the various departments we have
approx. 70 RS/6000s, plus some of damn near every other Unix box on
the planet (Siemens running SCO, HP9000s running HP-UX, various Suns
running Solaris in just about every variation of 2.x plus other
machines too numerous and strnage to mention). Hell, we even have one
RS/6000 (an E20) running NT! The RS/6000s are the least troublesome of
the bunch (the next less troublesome servers are the IBM PC750s
running OS/2 Warp 3. These machines run InterVoice's VRU software, run
24X7 and 3 of them have presently been running in excess of 1 YEAR
without a reboot).

Based on the experiences at my present work site, I would recommend
the RS/6000s running AIX. I'll admit to you up front that I'm not a
"Unix guru" by any stretch of the imagination, but I've seen the
numbers and the RS/6000 looks pretty good.

Regards,
  Bill Bowen
  bowenb@best.com
  Daly City, CA


